<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The incident occurred on 26 October 2023 at about 7 p.m., when the appellant was alleged to have sexually assaulted a ten-year-old girl. A child helpline call was made the same night, and the first information report was registered the next day at Devadhanapatti Police Station, District Theni (Crime No. 411 of 2023). The trial court — in Spl.S.C. No. 17 of 2024 — framed a charge under Section 9(m) read with Section 10 of the POCSO Act, examined seventeen witnesses, marked eleven exhibits, and convicted the appellant under Section 10, sentencing him to five years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with a fine of Rs. 10,000.</p>
<p>The appeal, filed under Section 415(2) of the BNSS, came before Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice D. Lingeswaran, who dismissed it on 12 November 2026 and confirmed both conviction and sentence.</p>

<h2>The four findings that decided the appeal</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No material delay.</strong> The same-night helpline call and next-day FIR, viewed against the father&rsquo;s absence from the home, left no delay capable of generating suspicion.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A competent, consistent child.</strong> The victim was found competent to depose, and her account was consistent on the material particulars.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Independent corroboration.</strong> An independent eyewitness (PW-4) described the hand-pulling and the assault in terms matching the victim&rsquo;s account, with PW-2 adding further support.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A self-defeating defence.</strong> The claim of enmity over an alleged illicit relationship found no support, and the fallback plea that the appellant &ldquo;only pushed&rdquo; the child sat uneasily beside it — courts treat inconsistent alternative defences as weakening both.</div>
</div>

<h2>The sentencing point: statutory minimums are floors, not suggestions</h2>
<p>Perhaps the most practically significant paragraph is the last: the Court held that it could not reduce the sentence below the five-year minimum that Section 10 prescribes. POCSO&rsquo;s sentencing scheme is built on legislatively fixed minimums precisely to remove the discretion that produced wildly divergent outcomes in child sexual assault cases. Appellate arguments about the appellant&rsquo;s age, family circumstances or the time elapsed since the offence can operate within the statutory range, never below it.</p>

<h2>Reading the case alongside the settled law</h2>
<p>The judgment applies, rather than extends, the Supreme Court&rsquo;s framework. <em>Ganesan v. State</em>, (2020) 10 SCC 573, settled that a survivor&rsquo;s sole testimony, if it inspires confidence, needs no corroboration as a matter of law. The prompt-complaint principle, anchored in Section 8, Illustration (j) of the Evidence Act, treats the immediacy of disclosure as conduct consistent with the truth of the accusation. Where, as here, both planks are present and an independent eyewitness stands with the child, an appellate court has little room to interfere — its jurisdiction is to correct error, not to re-live the trial.</p>
<p>One structural note for practitioners: the charge was framed under Section 9(m) read with Section 10 (aggravated sexual assault on a child below twelve), and the conviction was recorded under Section 10. Defence counsel examining such records should always verify that the charge, the findings and the conviction provision align, since misalignment can itself furnish a ground of appeal — though none was found material here.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information. Identities of victims in POCSO matters are protected by law and are not disclosed in court records or here.</div>
